From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Johannes Stein <johannesstein@freeze-dev.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 05:59:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 133/133] aarch64: relax cbz, cbnz and b.cond too, and count
 .lcomm and entry symbols

A rewrite adds a b, which moves the code after it, so a cbz, cbnz or
b.cond already at the edge of its +-1 MiB reach could be pushed past it.
Those are now rewritten the same way when out of reach (cbz <-> cbnz,
b.cond with the inverse condition), and the pass repeats until no
conditional branch is out of reach. That also covers a procedure larger
than 1 MiB.

The count now includes inline .lcomm and .comm, which some targets write in
place as .zero, and the labels include the symbols defined in the code, so
a b.cond to its own procedure by name is measured too.

ttbz2.pp takes its 36000 bytes of .ascii from ttbz2.inc and adds .lcomm, a
b.eq back to its procedure by name, cbz and b.eq across 1100000 bytes, and a
tbz exactly in reach (+32764) that only goes out of reach when the branch
inside it is rewritten, which only the second pass sees.
